WHAT THIS PROJECT USES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT (CC BY 4.0 section 3(a)(1)(B), indication of changes)
  USED: numbers only. The per-slice `NerveMorphology.csv` products of the six samples (columns `index`, `area`,
  `perimeter`, `eq_diameter`, `center_x`, `center_y`, `major_axis`, `minor_axis`, `angle`, all in VOXELS of the
  11.4 um scan) and the `sciatic_levels.csv` annotations, which give the slice index of five named anatomical
  levels per side: the level of formation of the sciatic nerve from the sacral roots, the level of the lesser
  trochanter of the femur, and +7 / +14 / +21 cm below it. Q304 converts those columns to millimetres
  (0.0114 mm per voxel) and reads the equivalent diameter at each named level, over a +-2 mm window of slices.
  Those derived numbers are in `data/derived/Q304_sparc692_calibre.json`.
  NOT USED: no geometry, no image, no mask, no mesh and no centreline shape from this dataset is shipped,
  displayed or derived from. The samples were EXCISED and laid straight in the scanner (Q302 measured it: SCL2's
  traced centreline spans 84.76 mm axially with an 85.09 mm arc length and 1.0 x 1.3 mm of lateral wander), so
  the dataset carries no in-body trajectory and may not be used for the shape of anything.
  NOT CHANGED BY US: nothing in this dataset. Q304 changed no mesh of this project either -- the comparison is
  reported on each sciatic nerve's card with the reasons it did not become a geometry change.
  NOT ENDORSED: neither the authors, Case Western Reserve University, Duke University, the SPARC Consortium nor
  the NIH endorse this project or its use of their measurements. The figures are one embalmed, PTA-stained,
  excised specimen (n = 1) and are a LOWER BOUND on a living nerve's calibre.
